+# CLAUDE.md
+
+This file gives Claude Code project-specific guidance for this repository.
+
+## Project overview
+
+Dragon-Ball-Z-Budokai is a Unity/C# fighting-game prototype inspired by Dragon Ball Z: Budokai. The current repo is small and script-focused, with Unity scene/assets metadata and core player/combat controller scripts at the repository root.
+
+## Tech stack
+
+- Unity project
+- C# scripts using `UnityEngine`
+- Visual Studio solution: `Dragonball Z Budokai.sln`
+
+## Repository structure
+
+- `DragonBallZBudokai.unity` — main Unity scene.
+- `PlayerMovement.cs` — main Budokai-style player controller, combat states, attack data, movement, guard, ki charge, teleport, combo buffering, hit reactions, and KO logic.
+- `FighterMovement.cs` — simple target-facing movement script.
+- `CameraController.cs`, `MidPoint.cs`, `AudioObject.cs`, `GameManager.cs` — supporting gameplay scripts.
+- `.github/workflows/` — GitHub Actions workflows.
+
+## Coding guidelines
+
+- Keep scripts compatible with Unity C# conventions.
+- Prefer clear serialized fields with `[Header]`, `[SerializeField]`, and Unity-friendly public configuration fields where Inspector tuning is intended.
+- Avoid hardcoding character-specific logic when data-driven `AttackData` or serialized references can be used.
+- Preserve existing gameplay concepts: Budokai-style auto-facing, sidestepping, guard, ki charging, teleporting, combo chains, launches, knockback, wall hits, and KO states.
+- Use coroutines for short timed gameplay sequences when consistent with the current codebase.
+- Do not rename public fields, animator parameters, Unity input names, scene objects, or serialized classes without considering prefab/scene serialization breakage.
+- When changing animator logic, document required Animator parameters and animation events in comments or README updates.
+- Keep null checks around optional Unity references such as opponent transforms, animators, and attack data.
+
+## Common Unity concerns
+
+- Unity serialization depends on class names, namespaces, and field names. Be cautious when refactoring `MonoBehaviour` classes.
+- `PlayerMovement.cs` currently declares `BudokaiPlayerController`; file/class name mismatches can confuse Unity workflows. Do not introduce more mismatches.
+- Prefer `GetComponent<T>()` over non-generic `GetComponent()`.
+- Avoid doing expensive object searches in `Update()`.
+- Use `Time.deltaTime` for frame-rate-independent movement.
+
+## Testing and validation
+
+There is no automated test suite currently documented. For gameplay changes, validate in Unity by checking:
+
+- The scene opens without missing script errors.
+- Player and opponent references are assigned.
+- Movement axes and input buttons match Unity Input Manager mappings.
+- Animator parameters used by scripts exist.
+- Combo windows fire through animation events: `EnableComboWindow`, `DisableComboWindow`, `OnAttackHit`, and `EndAttack`.
+- Hit reactions, launch, teleport, guard, ki charge, and KO states behave correctly during play mode.
+
+## Recommended tasks for future cleanup
+
+- Format C# files with standard multi-line C# formatting.
+- Add a real `README.md` with setup instructions, Unity version, controls, and known limitations.
+- Move scripts into a `Scripts/` folder if Unity metadata is updated safely.
+- Add defensive checks and editor-time validation for required references.
+- Consider replacing old Unity Input Manager calls with the new Input System if the project standardizes on it.
